From 3780b9f83df6db80d760c7275aea628a03a1009b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brian 'redbeard' Harrington <redbeard@dead-city.org>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 18:54:02 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] Platform/Spacemit/K3: ACPI 6.6 conformance fixes for the AIA
 tables

Addresses the 2026-11-20 audit (A1-A5, A7, A8). The AIA-specific
IMSIC/APLIC MADT values were confirmed correct; these are ACPI 6.6
conformance and description-quality defects:

- A1: re-add include ("Cpu.asl") to the DSDT. ACPI 6.6 5.2.12.27
  requires every RINTC hart to have a processor device object
  (_HID ACPI0007, _UID matching the RINTC AcpiProcessorUid). The
  monolithic-DSDT rewrite dropped it, so acpi_processor could not
  bind and cpuidle/_LPI (Task 9) had no namespace objects. Cpu.asl
  provides CPU0-7 plus CL00/CL01 ACPI0010 containers whose _UIDs
  match the PPTT cluster nodes.
- A2: APLIC NumExtIntSources 511 -> 512, matching
  riscv,num-sources = <512> in the vendored and OS K3.dtsi.
- A3: MADT revision EFI_ACPI_6_5 (0x06) -> EFI_ACPI_6_6 (0x07); the
  table carries 6.6 RINTC/IMSIC/APLIC subtypes and must self-identify
  as 6.6.
- A4: APLIC HardwareId 0 -> "SPMT0001" (ACPI 6.6 Table 5.59 requires
  a valid NNNN#### ACPI ID). New ACPI_HWID_LIST_8 macro passes the
  8 ASCII bytes through the INIT macro (macro arguments cannot carry
  unparenthesized comma lists).
- A5: device MMIO _CRS descriptors for IC00/COM0/ETH0/USB2 switch
  ResourceProducer -> ResourceConsumer; they are devices, not
  bridges. PCI0 windows and RES0 stay producers.
- A7: delete the dead DSDT split files (Aplic/Uart/Usb/Pci.asl).
  Dsdt.asl is the single source of truth; Aplic.asl declared a second
  IC00 and Uart.asl carried a stale _DEP, both traps if anyone
  re-included them.
- A8: STATIC_ASSERT that the explicit RINTC/RHCT-CPU initializer
  counts match PcdX100HartCount; a PCD change without updating the
  initializers would emit zero-filled subtables.

Deliberately NOT done (A6): no RSCV0006 RPMI SYSMSI device. The
firmware-side SYSMSI group has an unvalidated-MSI-target hole
(k3_os0_validate_msi_addr returns success unconditionally; a
shared-memory client could direct arbitrary aligned 32-bit writes)
plus response-format defects. Exposing it in ACPI would make that
reachable; it stays uninstantiated until the service is fixed.

Validation: full RELEASE build (ACPI_ENABLE=TRUE); tables decoded
from the built DXEFV: MADT revision 7, APLIC HwId=SPMT0001
NumSources=512 NumIdcs=0, 8 RINTC records with nonzero UIDs, DSDT
carries 8 ACPI0007 + 2 ACPI0010, four device descriptors decode as
ResourceConsumer while PCI0 keeps 3 producer windows. Hardware boot
validation pending (scheduled with the board owner).
